\h Galatians
\toc1 Galatians
\toc2 Galatians
\toc3 GAL
\mt1 Galatians
\c 1
\s1 Paul’s Greeting to the Galatians
\p \v 1 Paul, an apostle — \add sent\add* not from men nor by man, but by Jesus Christ and God \add the\add* Father, who raised Him from \add the\add* dead — \v 2 and all the brothers with me,
\p To the churches of Galatia:
\p \v 3 Grace and peace to you from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ,\f + \fr 1:3 \ft SBL, BYZ, and TR \fqa God the Father and our Lord Jesus Christ\f* \v 4 who gave Himself for our sins to rescue us from the present evil age, according to the will of our God and Father, \v 5 to whom \add be\add* glory forever \add and\add* ever. Amen.
\s1 No Other Gospel
\p \v 6 I am amazed how quickly you are deserting the \add One who\add* called you by \add the\add* grace of Christ \add and are turning\add* to a different gospel — \v 7 which is not even \add a gospel\add*. Evidently some \add people\add* are troubling you and trying to distort the gospel of Christ.
\p \v 8 But even if we or an angel from heaven should preach a gospel contrary to the one we preached to you, let him be under a curse! \v 9 As we have said before, so now I say again: If anyone is preaching to you a gospel contrary to the one you received, let him be under a curse!
\s1 Paul Preaches the Gospel
\p \v 10 \add Am I\add* now seeking the approval of men, or of God? Or am I striving to please men? If I were still trying to please men, I would not be a servant of Christ. \v 11 For I want you to know, brothers, \add that\add* the gospel I preached was not devised by man.\f + \fr 1:11 \ft Literally \fqa not according to man\f* \v 12 I did not receive it from any man, nor was I taught \add it\add*; rather, \add I received it \add* by revelation from Jesus Christ.
\p \v 13 For you have heard of my former way of life in Judaism, how severely I persecuted the church of God and tried to destroy it. \v 14 I was advancing in Judaism beyond many of my contemporaries \add and\add* was extremely zealous for the traditions of my fathers.
\p \v 15 But when God, who set me apart from my mother’s womb and called \add me\add* by His grace, was pleased \v 16 to reveal His Son in me so that I might preach Him among the Gentiles, I did not rush to consult with flesh and blood, \v 17 nor did I go up to Jerusalem to the apostles \add who came\add* before me, but I went into Arabia and \add later\add* returned to Damascus.
\p \v 18 Only after three years did I go up to Jerusalem to confer with Cephas,\f + \fr 1:18 \ft That is, Peter\f* and I stayed with him fifteen days. \v 19 But I saw none of the other apostles except James, the Lord’s brother. \v 20 I assure you before God that what I am writing to you is no lie.
\p \v 21 Later I went to the regions of Syria and Cilicia. \v 22 I was personally unknown, however, to the churches of Judea that \add are\add* in Christ. \v 23 They only heard the account: “The \add man who\add* formerly persecuted us \add is\add* now preaching the faith \add he\add* once tried to destroy.” \v 24 And they glorified God because of me.
\c 2
\s1 The Council at Jerusalem
\r (\ref Acts 15:5–21|ACT 15:5-21\ref*)
\p \v 1 Fourteen years later I went up again to Jerusalem, accompanied by Barnabas. I took Titus \add along\add* also. \v 2 I went in response to a revelation and set before them the gospel that I preach among the Gentiles. But \add I spoke\add* privately to those recognized as leaders, \add for fear that\add* I was running or had already run in vain. \v 3 Yet not even Titus, who \add was\add* with me, was compelled to be circumcised, \add even though\add* he was a Greek.
\p \v 4 \add This issue arose\add* because \add some\add* false brothers had come in under false pretenses to spy on our freedom in Christ Jesus, in order to enslave us. \v 5 We did \add not\add* give in to \add them\add* for a moment, so that the truth of the gospel would remain with you.
\p \v 6 But as for the highly esteemed— whatever they were makes no difference to me; God does not show favoritism\f + \fr 2:6 \ft Literally \fqa God does not accept the face of man\f*— those leaders added nothing to me. \v 7 On the contrary, they saw that I had been entrusted \add to preach\add* the gospel to the uncircumcised, just as Peter \add had been \add* to the circumcised. \v 8 For the \add One who\add* was at work in Peter’s apostleship to the circumcised was also at work in my \add apostleship\add* to the Gentiles.
\p \v 9 And recognizing the grace \add that\add* I had been given, James, Cephas,\f + \fr 2:9 \ft That is, Peter; also in verses 11 and 14\f* and John — those reputed to be pillars — gave me and Barnabas \add the\add* right hand of fellowship, so that we \add should go \add* to the Gentiles, and they to the circumcised. \v 10 \add They\add* only \add asked us\add* to remember the poor, the very thing I was eager to do.
\s1 Paul Confronts Cephas
\p \v 11 When Cephas came to Antioch, however, I opposed \add him\add* to his face, because he stood condemned. \v 12 For before certain men came from James, he used to eat with the Gentiles. But when they arrived, he began to draw back and separate himself, for fear of those in \add the\add* circumcision \add group \add*. \v 13 The other Jews joined him in his hypocrisy, so that by their hypocrisy even Barnabas was led astray.
\p \v 14 When I saw that they were not walking in line with the truth of the gospel, I said to Cephas in front of \add them\add* all, “If you, who are a Jew, live like a Gentile and not like a Jew, how can you compel the Gentiles to live like Jews?”\f + \fr 2:14 \ft Some translators close this quotation after verse 16 or 21.\f*
\p \v 15 We \add who are\add* Jews by birth and not Gentile “sinners” \v 16 know that a man is not justified by works of \add the\add* law, but by faith in Jesus Christ. So we, too, have believed in Christ Jesus, that we may be justified by faith in Christ and not by works of the law, because by works of the law no one will be justified.
\p \v 17 But if, while we seek to be justified in Christ, we ourselves are found to be sinners, does that make Christ a minister of sin? Certainly not! \v 18 If I rebuild what I have already torn down, I prove myself \add to be\add* a lawbreaker.
\p \v 19 For through \add the\add* law I died to \add the\add* law so that I might live to God. \v 20 I have been crucified with Christ, and I no longer live, but Christ lives in me. The \add life\add* I live in \add the\add* body, I live by faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave Himself up for me. \v 21 I do not set aside the grace of God. For if righteousness \add comes\add* through the law, then Christ died for nothing.
\c 3
\s1 Faith and Belief
\r (\ref James 2:14–26|JAS 2:14-26\ref*)
\p \v 1 O foolish Galatians! Who has bewitched you? Before \add your very\add* eyes Jesus Christ was clearly portrayed \add as\add* crucified. \v 2 I would like to learn just \add one thing\add* from you: Did you receive the Spirit by works of the law, or by hearing with faith?
\p \v 3 Are you so foolish? After starting in \add the\add* Spirit, are you now finishing in \add the\add* flesh? \v 4 Have you suffered so much for nothing, if it really was for nothing? \v 5 \add Does God\add* lavish \add His\add* Spirit on you and work miracles among you because \add you\add* practice the law, or because \add you\add* hear \add and\add* believe?
\p \v 6 So also, “Abraham believed God, and it was credited to him as righteousness.”\f + \fr 3:6 \ref Genesis 15:6|GEN 15:6\ref*\f* \v 7 Understand, then, that those who have faith are sons of Abraham. \v 8 The Scripture foresaw that God would justify the Gentiles by faith, \add and\add* foretold the gospel to Abraham: “All nations will be blessed through you.”\f + \fr 3:8 \ft See \ref Genesis 12:3|GEN 12:3\ref*, Genesis 18:18, and Genesis 22:18.\f* \v 9 So those who have faith are blessed along with Abraham, the \add man\add* of faith.
\s1 Christ Has Redeemed Us
\p \v 10 All who rely on works of the law are under a curse. For it is written: “Cursed is everyone who does not continue to do everything written in the Book of the Law.”\f + \fr 3:10 \ref Deuteronomy 27:26|DEU 27:26\ref* (see also LXX)\f* \v 11 Now it is clear that no one is justified before God by \add the\add* law, because, “The righteous will live by faith.”\f + \fr 3:11 \ref Habakkuk 2:4|HAB 2:4\ref*\f* \v 12 The law, however, is not based on faith; on the contrary, “The \add man who\add* does these things will live by them.”\f + \fr 3:12 \ref Leviticus 18:5|LEV 18:5\ref*; see also Ezekiel 20:11, 13, and 21.\f*
\p \v 13 Christ redeemed us from the curse of the law by becoming a curse for us. For it is written: “Cursed is everyone who is hung on a tree.”\f + \fr 3:13 \ref Deuteronomy 21:23|DEU 21:23\ref* (see also LXX)\f* \v 14 \add He redeemed us\add* in order that the blessing promised to Abraham\f + \fr 3:14 \ft Literally \fqa the blessing of Abraham\f* would come to the Gentiles in Christ Jesus, so that by faith we might receive the promise of the Spirit.
\s1 The Purpose of the Law
\r (\ref Romans 7:1–6|ROM 7:1-6\ref*)
\p \v 15 Brothers, let me put this in human terms. Even a human covenant, once it is ratified, cannot be canceled or amended. \v 16 The promises were spoken to Abraham and to his seed. \add The Scripture \add* does not say, “and to seeds,” meaning many, but “and to your seed,”\f + \fr 3:16 \ref Genesis 12:7|GEN 12:7\ref*; Genesis 13:15\f* meaning One, who is Christ.
\p \v 17 What I mean \add is\add* this: \add The\add* law that came 430 years later does not revoke \add the\add* covenant previously established by God, so as to nullify the promise. \v 18 For if the inheritance depends on \add the\add* law, \add then it\add* no longer depends on a promise; but God freely granted \add it\add* to Abraham through a promise.
\p \v 19 Why then  \add was \add* the law \add given \add*? It was added because of transgressions, until \add the\add* arrival of the seed to whom \add the\add* promise referred. It was administered through angels by a mediator. \v 20 A mediator is unnecessary, however,  \add for only \add* one \add party \add*; but God is one.
\p \v 21 \add Is\add* the law, then, opposed to the promises of God? Certainly not! For if a law had been given that could impart life, then righteousness \add would\add* certainly have come from \add the\add* law. \v 22 But the Scripture  \add pronounces \add* all things confined by sin, so that by faith in Jesus Christ the promise might be given to those who believe.
\p \v 23 Before \add this\add* faith came, we were held in custody under the law, locked up until faith should be revealed. \v 24 So the law became our guardian to lead us to Christ, that we might be justified by faith. \v 25 Now \add that\add* faith has come, we are no longer under a guardian.
\s1 Sons through Faith in Christ
\p \v 26 You are all sons of God through faith in Christ Jesus. \v 27 For all of you \add who\add* were baptized into Christ have clothed yourselves with Christ. \v 28 There is neither Jew nor Greek, slave nor free, male \add nor\add* female, for you are all one in Christ Jesus. \v 29 And if you belong to Christ, then you are Abraham’s seed \add and\add* heirs according to \add the\add* promise.
\c 4
\s1 Sons and Heirs
\p \v 1 \add What\add* I am saying \add is that\add* as long as the heir is a child, he is no different from a slave, \add although\add* he is the owner of everything. \v 2 He is subject to guardians and trustees until the date set by \add his\add* father.
\p \v 3 So also, when we were children, we were enslaved under the basic principles\f + \fr 4:3 \ft Or \fqa elemental forces\ft ; similarly in verse 9\f* of the world. \v 4 But when the time \add had\add* fully come, God sent His Son, born of a woman, born under \add the\add* law, \v 5 to redeem those under \add the\add* law, that we might receive \add our\add* adoption as sons. \v 6 And because you are sons, God sent the Spirit of His Son into our hearts, crying out, “Abba, Father!” \v 7 So you are no longer a slave, but a son; and since \add you are\add* a son, \add you are\add* also an heir through God.
\s1 Paul’s Concern for the Galatians
\p \v 8 Formerly, when you did not know God, you were slaves to those who by nature are not gods. \v 9 But now that you know God, or rather are known by God, how \add is it that\add* you are turning back to those weak and worthless principles? Do you wish to be enslaved by \add them\add* all over again? \v 10 You are observing \add special\add* days and months and seasons and years! \v 11 I fear for you, \add that\add* my efforts for you may have been in vain. \v 12 I beg you, brothers, become like me, for I became like you. You have done me no wrong.
\p \v 13 You know that it was because of an illness \add that\add* I first preached the gospel to you. \v 14 And \add although\add* my \add illness was a\add* trial to you, you did not despise \add or\add* reject \add me\add*. Instead, you welcomed me as if \add I were\add* an angel of God, as if \add I were\add* Christ Jesus \add Himself\add*. \v 15 What then \add has become\add* of your blessing? For I can testify that, if \add it were\add* possible, you would have torn out your eyes \add and\add* given \add them\add* to me. \v 16 Have I now become your enemy \add by\add* telling you the truth?
\p \v 17 \add Those people\add* are zealous for you, \add but\add* not in a good way. Instead, they want to isolate you \add from us\add*, so that you may be zealous for them. \v 18 Nevertheless, \add it is\add* good to be zealous if it serves a noble \add purpose\add* — at any time, and not only when I am with you.
\p \v 19 My children, for whom I am again in the pains of childbirth until Christ is formed in you, \v 20 \add how\add* I wish I could be with you now and change my tone, because I am perplexed about you.
\s1 Hagar and Sarah
\r (\ref Genesis 21:9–21|GEN 21:9-21\ref*)
\p \v 21 Tell me, \add you\add* who want to be under \add the\add* law, do you not understand \add what\add* the law \add says\add*? \v 22 For it is written that Abraham had two sons, one by the slave woman and \add the other\add* by the free \add woman\add*.\f + \fr 4:22 \ft See \ref Genesis 16:15|GEN 16:15\ref* and Genesis 21:2–3.\f* \v 23 His \add son\add* by the slave woman was born according to \add the\add* flesh, \add but his son\add* by the free \add woman was born\add* through the promise.
\p \v 24 These \add things\add* serve as illustrations, for \add the women\add* represent two covenants. One \add covenant is\add* from Mount Sinai \add and bears\add* children into slavery: This is Hagar. \v 25 Now Hagar stands for Mount Sinai in Arabia and corresponds to the present-day Jerusalem, because she is in slavery with her children. \v 26 But the Jerusalem above is free, \add and she\add* is our mother. \v 27 For it is written:
\b 
\q1 “Rejoice, \add O\add* barren woman,
\q2 who bears no children;
\q1 break forth and cry aloud,
\q2 \add you\add* who \add have\add* never travailed;
\q1 because more \add are\add* the children of the desolate woman
\q2 than of her who has a husband.”\f + \fr 4:27 \ref Isaiah 54:1|ISA 54:1\ref*\f*
\p \v 28 Now you,\f + \fr 4:28 \ft WH, BYZ, and TR \fqa we\f* brothers, like Isaac, are children of promise. \v 29 At that time, however, the \add son\add* born by \add the\add* flesh persecuted the \add son born\add* by \add the\add* Spirit. \add It is\add* the same now.
\p \v 30 But what \add does\add* the Scripture say? “Expel the slave woman and her son, for the slave woman’s son will never share in the inheritance with the free \add woman’s\add* son.”\f + \fr 4:30 \ref Genesis 21:10|GEN 21:10\ref*\f* \v 31 Therefore, brothers, we are not children of \add the\add* slave woman, but of the free \add woman\add*.
\c 5
\s1 Freedom in Christ
\p \v 1 \add It is\add* for freedom \add that\add* Christ has set us free. Stand firm, then, and \add do\add* not be encumbered once more by a yoke of slavery.
\p \v 2 Take notice: I, Paul, tell you that if you let yourselves be circumcised, Christ will be of no value to you \add at all\add*. \v 3 Again I testify to every man who gets himself circumcised that he is obligated to obey the whole law. \v 4 \add You\add* who are \add trying\add* to be justified by \add the\add* law have been severed from Christ; you have fallen away from grace.
\p \v 5 \add But\add* by faith we eagerly await through \add the\add* Spirit \add the\add* hope of righteousness. \v 6 For in Christ Jesus neither circumcision nor uncircumcision has any value. What matters is faith expressing itself through love.
\p \v 7 You were running so well. Who has obstructed you from obeying the truth? \v 8 \add Such\add* persuasion \add does\add* not \add come\add* from the \add One\add* who calls you. \v 9 A little leaven works through the whole batch \add of dough\add*. \v 10 I am confident in \add the\add* Lord that you will take no other view. The \add one who is\add* troubling you will bear the judgment, whoever he may be.
\p \v 11 Now, brothers, if I \add am\add* still preaching circumcision, why am I still being persecuted? In that case the offense of the cross has been abolished. \v 12 As for those who are agitating you, I wish they would proceed to emasculate themselves!
\p \v 13 For you, brothers, were called to freedom; but \add do\add* not \add use your\add* freedom as an opportunity for the flesh. Rather, serve one another in love. \v 14 The entire law is fulfilled in a single decree: “Love your neighbor as yourself.”\f + \fr 5:14 \ref Leviticus 19:18|LEV 19:18\ref*\f* \v 15 But if you keep on biting and devouring one another, watch out, \add or\add* you will be consumed by one another.
\s1 Walking by the Spirit
\r (\ref Ezekiel 36:16–38|EZK 36:16-38\ref*; \ref Romans 8:9–11|ROM 8:9-11\ref*)
\p \v 16 So I say, walk by \add the\add* Spirit, and you will not gratify \add the\add* desires \add of the\add* flesh. \v 17 For the flesh craves what is contrary to the Spirit, and the Spirit what is contrary to the flesh. \add They\add* are opposed to each other, so that you do not do what you want. \v 18 But if you are led by \add the\add* Spirit, you are not under \add the\add* law.
\p \v 19 The acts of the flesh are obvious: sexual immorality, impurity, \add and\add* debauchery; \v 20 idolatry \add and\add* sorcery; hatred, discord, jealousy, \add and\add* rage; rivalries, divisions, factions, \v 21 \add and\add* envy; drunkenness, orgies, and the like. I warn you, as I did before, that those who practice such things will not inherit \add the\add* kingdom of God.
\p \v 22 But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, \v 23 gentleness, \add and\add* self-control. Against such things there is no law.
\p \v 24 Those who \add belong to\add* Christ Jesus have crucified the flesh with \add its\add* passions and desires. \v 25 Since we live by \add the\add* Spirit, let us walk in step with \add the\add* Spirit. \v 26 Let us not become conceited, provoking \add and\add* envying one another.
\c 6
\s1 Carry One Another’s Burdens
\p \v 1 Brothers, if someone is caught in a trespass, you who are spiritual should restore him with a spirit of gentleness. \add But\add* watch yourself, or you also may be tempted. \v 2 Carry one another’s burdens, and in this way you will fulfill the law of Christ.
\p \v 3 If anyone thinks he is something when he is nothing, he deceives himself.
\p \v 4 Each one should test his own work. Then he will have reason to boast in himself alone, and not in someone else. \v 5 For each one should carry his own load. \v 6 Nevertheless, the one who receives instruction in the word must share in all good things with \add his\add* instructor.
\p \v 7 \add Do\add* not be deceived: God \add is\add* not to be mocked. Whatever a man sows, he will reap in return. \v 8 The \add one\add* who sows to please his flesh, from the flesh will reap destruction; but the \add one\add* who sows to please the Spirit, from the Spirit will reap eternal life.
\p \v 9 Let us not grow weary \add in\add* well-doing, for in due time we will reap a harvest if we do not give up. \v 10 Therefore, as we have opportunity, let us do good to everyone, and especially to the family of faith.
\s1 Final Warnings and Blessings
\p \v 11 See \add what\add* large letters I am using to write to you with my own hand!
\p \v 12 Those who want to make a good impression outwardly are trying to compel you to be circumcised. \add They\add* only \add do this \add* to avoid persecution for the cross of Christ. \v 13 For the circumcised \add do\add* not even keep \add the\add* law themselves, yet they want you to be circumcised that they may boast in your flesh.
\p \v 14 But as for me, may \add I\add* never boast, except in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ, through which\f + \fr 6:14 \ft Or \fqa through whom\f* \add the\add* world has been crucified to me, and I \add to the\add* world. \v 15 For neither circumcision nor uncircumcision means anything. \add What counts is\add* a new creation.
\p \v 16 Peace and mercy to all who walk by this rule, even to the Israel of God.
\p \v 17 From now on \add let\add* no one cause me trouble, for I bear on my body the marks of Jesus.
\p \v 18 The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ \add be\add* with your spirit, brothers.
\p Amen.
